host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title A tool proven on one shape
prompt Autonomous loop, second increment -- take the named next crux (the four-room index fold) now that its meter reads the law rather than a copy
think The first increment landed and pushed, so the next crux is the one three logs in a row have named: the index fold for four rooms, each its own round. Lindy-first among them is active-designing -- the essays room, the one a reader opens most often of the four.
think Before folding, I read the tool's own link-depth rule rather than trusting it. shelf_row prepends ../ to every relative target that is not date/..., so a row pointing at yonder/ resolves correctly from a shelf one directory down. That part was already general, and its comment names the 62 outward-reaching targets it was written for.
think The fold ran and I read the front door it produced. The two kept rows had landed below the closing benediction, outside any table. Root: fold_index collects kept rows into a second buffer and appends them after walking all prose.
think Why that shipped: it is the repair session-logs needed once, when its title stood at line 1,881 beneath 1,880 rows (REDS %182), and session-logs/README.md has its table as the last block. Appending at the end and keeping in place are the same write there. The selftest fixture was shaped the same way, so nothing could tell them apart. Booked as REDS %200.
think The repair is one line: write a kept row where it was read. The proof is a fixture that differs from the subject -- a closing line beneath the table -- and an assertion comparing the kept row's offset against that line's.
think Then a fault of my own making. A fold removes the rows between a table and what follows, so a heading can abut the last surviving row; I restored the consumed blank line keyed off whether the fold could date the row. That put a blank BETWEEN two table rows in the fixture, since a table also holds header rows, delimiter rows, living rows carrying no stamp, and day-only rows -- all read as prose, correctly, having no day to file them under. A blank inside a table ends the table. Worse than the cosmetic fault it was fixing, and the selftest caught it before it reached a room.
think The honest test is a leading pipe, which is what GFM itself reads. Keyed that way, the fixture keeps its table whole and the closing rule gets its blank.
think Third finding in the same fold: every shelf link resolved except ../SEASONS.md, the header's roster pointer. One room in this tree keeps that page, so the first fold wrote 21 shelves each carrying a link to a file that was never there -- and a shelf is immutable once written, so no later pass may repair it in place. The header asks the room now with path_exists.
obs Four selftest legs added, each proven on metal from both sides by putting the elder behavior back and watching the named error fire: SelftestKeptRowLeftItsTable, SelftestBlankSplitTheTable, SelftestHeadingAbutsTable, and SelftestShelfNamesAbsentRoster beside SelftestShelfLostItsPinLink. The third leg needed the split check to read BEFORE the abut check to be reachable at all, which is also the right order -- a split table stops rendering where an abutting heading only reads poorly.
obs The transferable rule is about fixtures rather than about folds: a fixture built to match its one subject proves the tool on that subject. This fixture's table was last because the room's table was last, so the shape the tool would meet everywhere else was never in the pen.
obs Rye toolchain fact for the next lap: rye run takes its script arguments after a -- separator, and this bench resolves the toolchain through RYE_ZIG=$(pwd)/vendor/zig-toolchain/zig (zig 0.16.0). Without the separator, zig reads the first argument as a filename and refuses.
obs active-designing joins session-logs on the ENFORCE roster, which the stamp-and-name law says a room earns by being carried across with its front door read afterwards. Its 86 rows sit on 21 shelves and every link in them resolves, checked by walking each target.
obs The round's own guards caught the round twice. readme_reach refused construction/REDS.md because row %200 reproduced the shelf header's link in full, Markdown brackets and all -- a quoted link is still a link, and it resolved from construction/ to a root-level page that has never existed. I then fixed it by writing the same link again inside the sentence describing the fault, and the guard refused a second time. The row describes the link now rather than writing it.
obs dated_path read 188 lost references against a ceiling of 187, and the one new arrival was my own selftest's planted row naming a log that existed nowhere. A selftest leaves its sandbox on the filesystem and dated_path_scan.sh reads the filesystem rather than the index, so a pen reference that resolves nowhere is a real entry in the tree's census. The planted log is written under date/20260702/ now and its shelf link resolves like any other.
obs Standfast held twice in this increment: each red stopped the line, was rooted, and closed on a guard on metal before the next step. The roster ran hot four times because of it, and the fourth read 50 of 50.
loom fold=active-designing rows_folded=86 rows_kept=2 shelves=21 days_seen=22 days_folded=21 pin_bytes_before=30748 pin_bytes_after=8565
loom guard=index_fold ratchet_before=317 ratchet_after=231 enforced_rooms=2 gated_stale=0
file tools/rye/session_logs_archive.rye fold_index keeps rows in place, restores the blank a fold consumes keyed off a leading pipe, and asks the room whether it keeps a seasons roster
file tools/fixtures/session_logs_archive/README.md a closing line beneath the table and a heading abutting it, so the fixture differs from its subject
file tools/fixtures/session_logs_archive/SEASONS.md the roster page the shelf header links to when a room keeps one
file tools/fixtures/index_fold_scan.sh active-designing enforced, ratchet ceiling 317 -> 231
file tools/fixtures/index_fold_control.sh planted counts follow the ceiling, 231 and 232
file tools/i/index_fold_witness.rish the second enforced room, the new ratchet, and the fault the first round found
file active-designing/README.md 86 rows folded out, front door 30,748 -> 8,565 bytes, table whole
file construction/REDS.md row %200 booked and closed, headline moved with it
file construction/ITINERARY.md Now refilled, one Prior laps row added and the oldest dropped
recommend keep-going three rooms remain and each is its own round -- counsel 112 stale rows, expanding-prompts 78, waymarks 41, 231 together under a ceiling that only falls. counsel is the largest and the room is closed, so its fold is pure carry; expanding-prompts sits 27 bytes over its declared bound and one fold brings it under. Beside them, a thing this lap saw and left measured rather than remembered: the REDS headline is one sentence that grows a clause per fold and now narrates seventeen of them, which is a list of what is on disk written by hand -- the roster shape %199 booked for the fifth time, and reading it from the archive filenames would retire the sentence.
